Simple linear laceration from broken glass during cleaning to palmar aspect of hand
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Cleanly incised palmar wound, Brisk compressible bleeding, Broken glass handling mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-021
Reason for Visit: Acute, briskly bleeding palmar wound requiring urgent hemostasis and exclusion of deep tendon/nerve laceration.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute palmar laceration with glass, presenting with significant bleeding. Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to clear deep space structures, evaluate for retained foreign bodies, and perform definitive primary closure to prevent severe functional morbidity and infection prior to the next available daytime consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I was washing a glass, it shattered, and sliced my hand open; it's bleeding heavily.
History of Present Illness: Patient was manually pressing firmly against shattered glassware during cleaning, resulting in an immediate cleanly incised linear wound. The wound penetrates the thick palmar epidermis and dermis. Patient noted brisk bleeding from the highly vascular palmar surface that responded rapidly to firm, continuous pressure. The initial event was followed by sharp, stinging pain instantly localized to the precise line of the cut. Patient denies any numbness, tingling, or inability to move the fingers.
Functional Status: Maintaining manual pressure; unable to utilize the affected hand.
Social/Safety Concerns: Shattered glass remains in the kitchen sink, presenting a secondary hazard.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/84 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Alert, mildly anxious, applying firm pressure to a blood-soaked towel on the hand.
Physical Exam: Palmar examination reveals a 3cm cleanly incised wound. Visualization demonstrates superficial subcutaneous fat without violation of the deep palmar aponeurosis. Rigorous wound exploration under local anesthesia confirms a total absence of deep space structural exposure or gritty foreign body sensation upon gentle probing. Brisk compressible bleeding is noted but achieves complete hemostasis. Active range of motion is preserved in all digits without pain upon resisted flexor testing. Normal two-point discrimination is confirmed on the volar pads of all affected fingers. Distal digital capillary refill occurs in less than two seconds.
Environmental Observations: Kitchen sink area contains shattered glass; patient advised on safe disposal later.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine anticoagulants.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Simple linear laceration to palmar aspect of hand - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Flexor tendon laceration - Ruled out due to: Full, pain-free active flexion of all digits against resistance.

Digital nerve laceration - Ruled out due to: Normal two-point discrimination on both the radial and ulnar aspects of all distal digital pads.

Retained foreign body (glass) - Ruled out due to: Thorough bedside exploration and high-pressure irrigation yielding a visually and tactilely clear wound bed.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Palmar lacerations caused by broken glass carry an inherently high risk of penetrating deep structures and retaining foreign bodies. Meticulous bedside exploration under local anesthesia confidently cleared the flexor tendons and digital nerves, classifying this as a simple dermal laceration. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically required to execute necessary high-volume decontamination, perform structural clearance, and achieve primary closure to halt bleeding and prevent functional impairment, successfully averting an unnecessary emergency department visit overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered local anesthesia. Performed thorough wound exploration and high-pressure saline irrigation. Primary closure achieved with 4-0 non-absorbable synthetic sutures. Tetanus prophylaxis updated.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep the hand elevated above the heart using a sling or pillows when resting. This utilizes gravity to decrease local venous pressure, directly stopping the throbbing pain and preventing the sutures from bursting under tension.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Simple non-adherent dressing and protective bandage.
Follow-up: Routine GP or practice nurse review in 10-14 days for suture removal.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department immediately if the bleeding restarts and does not stop after 15 minutes of heavy direct pressure, if the fingers become completely numb, or if you lose the ability to bend them.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Microscopic glass shards can occasionally evade clinical detection; return for review if a persistent hard, painful lump forms under the scar.
Clinical Course: Mild stinging will persist for 24-48 hours. Palmar sutures remain in place for 10-14 days due to the thick nature of the skin and high tension in the hand.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if red streaks track up your arm, or if the wound starts discharging thick yellow pus.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Sutures act as a mechanical bridge, holding the edges of the thick skin tightly together so the body can lay down a solid collagen scar.
Actionable Steps: Do not submerge the hand in dishwater or baths. Keep the dressing completely dry for the first 48 hours. Elevate the hand to reduce throbbing.

